<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip III of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1306">1306</a>–<a href="/years/1343">1343</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Évreux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip III (1306–1343), count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1328 jointly with his wife Joan II, daughter of Louis X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Their accession restored a separate Navarrese monarchy after decades of union with France. Philip carried out a revision of the kingdom's laws in 1330 and died in 1343 after taking part in the Castilian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Philip III of Navarre" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acoma · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippe_III_de_Navarre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born on 27 March 1306, the son of Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a>, and founder of the Capetian House of Évreux. In 1318 he married his cousin Joan, the daughter of Louis X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, who had died in 1316 leaving a four-year-old daughter. Philip V took the crown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> instead, and an agreement of 27 March 1318 betrothed Joan to Philip of Évreux and gave her a dowry. Philip inherited Évreux in Normandy in 1319, and the marriage was not consummated until 1324.</p><p>When Charles IV died on 1 February 1328 the direct male line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a> ended. Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip of Valois</a> were the strongest Capetian candidates, and the 35-year-old Philip of Valois was preferred over the 23-year-old Philip of Évreux because of his age. Philip VI took Philip of Évreux onto his council, and Philip and Joan ceded Champagne and Brie to him for the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, Mortain and Longueville.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>'s death ended a union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> of 44 years. The Navarrese assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puente-la-reina">Puente la Reina</a> elected regents on 13 March 1328 and recognised Joan as sovereign in May. Philip was at first ignored, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> confirmed him as king on 22 August, and both were crowned and raised on the shield in Pamplona Cathedral on 5 March 1329, the first Navarrese monarchs to be crowned. A charter of May 1329 stated that Joan was the true and natural heir but that the kingdom would obey her consort as king, and that both would renounce the crown when their heir reached twenty-one.</p><p>Philip proved an effective ruler, and his best-known achievement was the improvement of the kingdom's law code in 1330. The couple lived mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> but visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> several times. They built an irrigation system at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, repaired castles, and condemned the perpetrators of anti-Jewish riots and compensated the victims. In August 1328 Philip fought for Philip VI at the battle of Cassel, and in 1339 and 1340 he served in the opening campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. A war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> that began in 1335 ended with the treaty of Las Cuevas on 28 February 1336.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and arrived at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> in July 1343 with 100 knights and 300 infantrymen and a large quantity of provisions. In early September he fell ill, possibly after being wounded by an arrow, withdrew from the siege and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerez-de-la-frontera">Jerez de la Frontera</a> on 16 September 1343. His body was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and his heart in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Joan reigned alone until her death in 1349, when their son Charles II succeeded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip III of Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerez-de-la-frontera">Jerez de la Frontera</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.7,-6.12&amp;year=1330" aria-label="Jerez de la Frontera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4396 841 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Philip III of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4846 1111h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4846 1111h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.7,-6.12&amp;year=1330">What was here in 1330? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
